A||||5||Carl22||Cutting AC lines instead ||||5||
Z||000000||Carl22||05-13-2001||10:11 PM||||Has anyone opted for this option.  I know it sounds crazy, but it would be so much easier. I'm pulling a dead V6 engine out with cradle and all. (first time)  No gas in AC. Would it be easier to just cut the lines along the back engine wall then reconnecting them with a union clamped? That compressor area is not the most user friendly area. ||||reg||5
Z||000001||Raydar||05-13-2001||10:18 PM||||I think you can disconnect the lines where they leave the engine compartment, headed for the front of the car. If not, they can be disconnected at the back of the compressor. I wouldn't cut the lines. If you can't fix 'em, they're expensive to replace.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>88 black Formula<BR>88 Duke coupe <BR>88 "wadded up" Formula (parts car)||||reg||1
Z||000002||DavidHenshaw||05-13-2001||10:42 PM||||I dont know why but I discovered the AC lines on the GT I bought last fall had been cut. I wish they hadn't done that because it gets hot here in the summer. <P>Dave<P>86 GT||||reg||1
Z||000003||Taijiguy||05-13-2001||11:32 PM||||The hoses can be disconnected with a single bolt on the drivers side, forward engine bay at the bottom. There's an aluminum manifold, the parts are held together with a single 10 mm self tapping bolt.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Carl22||05-14-2001||06:44 AM||||Thanks, guys for the input.  I'll take a look at that manifold area.  I believe I already inspected, and all it looked like was a retaining place for the two hoses.  This car is a 87GT. I would think that this manifold is common for all. It would really make sense if the lines split there. I'm assuming that bolt is on the bottom.  Dave, I could see why those lines are cut.  That's the first thing that came to me upon inspecting possible disconnecting points.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Taijiguy||05-14-2001||08:06 AM||||Actually, the bolt head faces into the engine compartment...it's accessed from the top.||||reg||1
